  "content": {
    "id": "p131-task34-39-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "Determine whether the following functions are odd, even, or neither.",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p131-task34-39-part-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=2x^2+3x^4$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Even.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}f(-x)&=2(-x)^2+3(-x)^4\\\\f(-x)&=2x^2+3x^4\\\\f(-x)&=f(x)\\end{align*}$$\n\nThis holds for every real $x$, so the function is even.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      },
      {
        "id": "p131-task34-39-part-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=\\sqrt{x^2-x}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p131-task34-39-part-b-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Neither.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p131-task34-39-part-b-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "f(1)=0",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " but ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "f(-1)=\\sqrt{2}",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ", so neither defining identity holds.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "The real domain is $(-\\infty,0]\\cup[1,\\infty)$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}f(-x)&=\\sqrt{(-x)^2-(-x)}\\\\f(-x)&=\\sqrt{x^2+x}\\end{align*}$$\n\nBoth $1$ and $-1$ are in the domain. Since $f(1)=0$ and $f(-1)=\\sqrt{2}$, we have $f(-1)\\ne f(1)$ and $f(-1)\\ne-f(1)$. The function is neither even nor odd.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      },
      {
        "id": "p131-task34-39-part-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=x^2-4x+4$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Neither.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}f(-x)&=(-x)^2-4(-x)+4\\\\f(-x)&=x^2+4x+4\\end{align*}$$\n\nFor example, $f(1)=1$ and $f(-1)=9$. Thus $f(-1)$ equals neither $f(1)$ nor $-f(1)$, so the function is neither even nor odd.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      },
      {
        "id": "p131-task34-39-part-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=\\frac{x}{x^2-4}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Odd; $x\\ne\\pm2$.",
          "worked": "The domain excludes $x=\\pm2$ and is symmetric about $0$. For $x\\ne\\pm2$,\n\n$$\\begin{align*}f(-x)&=\\frac{-x}{(-x)^2-4}\\\\f(-x)&=-\\frac{x}{x^2-4}\\\\f(-x)&=-f(x)\\end{align*}$$\n\nTherefore, the function is odd.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 46
      },
      {
        "id": "p131-task34-39-part-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=x(x-1)^2$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Neither.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}f(-x)&=(-x)(-x-1)^2\\\\f(-x)&=-x(x+1)^2\\end{align*}$$\n\nFor example, $f(1)=0$ and $f(-1)=-4$. Thus $f(-1)$ equals neither $f(1)$ nor $-f(1)$, so the function is neither even nor odd.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      },
      {
        "id": "p131-task34-39-part-f",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "f",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=|x-1|$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Neither.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}f(-x)&=|-x-1|\\\\f(-x)&=|x+1|\\end{align*}$$\n\nFor example, $f(1)=0$ and $f(-1)=2$. Thus $f(-1)$ equals neither $f(1)$ nor $-f(1)$, so the function is neither even nor odd.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      }
    ]
  },
